     "<br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"auto\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Thursday, August 8, 2024 at 2:41:53 PM UTC-7 Joshua Heishman wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">Hello,<br><br>I am working on an age-structured Jolly Seber model in marginalized multistate parameterization (following Saracco &amp; Yackulic 2023) which is a based on the multistate JS described in Kery &amp; Schaub (2011).  My question relates specifically to defining the priors on entry (transition) probability within this age-structured framework.  Here&#39;s a generalized description of the problem:<br><br>Some critters exist in latent states (not-yet-entered, age-1, age-2, age-3, dead) with four observed states (seen as age-1, seen as age-2, seen as age-3, not observed).  Ages are known for all observed individuals.  All critters start in a dummy occasion in &quot;not-yet-entered&quot; state and then enter the observed states during sampling occasions.  Individuals can enter the population at any age.  To disregard the age-stratification briefly, entry probability at time t could be described in JAGS with a uniform distribution for simplicity as:<br><br>for (t in 1:(n.occasions-1)) {<br><span style=\"white-space:pre\">\t</span>gamma[t] ~ dunif(0, 1)<br>}<br><br>However, this entry probability describes the probability of entering the population inclusive of being in states age-1, age-2, or age-3.  The probability of entering the focal population at one of these given ages must be some component of the overall probability currently described in gamma above.  I have sought to define state-specific entry probabilities through time with an added dimension:<br><br>for (t in 1:(n.occasions-1)) {<br><span style=\"white-space:pre\">\t</span>for (s in 1:n.states) {<br><span style=\"white-space:pre\">\t\t</span>gamma[s, t] ~ # ?????<br><span style=\"white-space:pre\">\t</span>}<br>}<br><br>The distribution used for state-specific priors is especially important for determining the probability that an individual remains in the not-yet-entered (NYE) state at time t in a latent-state transition matrix:<br><br>for (t in 1:(n.occasions-1)) {<br><span style=\"white-space:pre\">\t</span>omega[NYE, NYE, t] &lt;- 1 - sum(gamma[1:3, t])<br>}<br><br>How could an uninformative prior on time-specific entry be spread across all latent states such that the time-specific probability of entry does not exceed 1?  Paper references or other demo sources appreciated.<div><br></div><div><div>Cheers,</div><div>Joshua</div></div></blockquote></div>"
